        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Menards Driveway Sealer",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/driveway-sealer-menards/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which Menards sealer should most Midwest blacktop owners choose?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a sound asphalt driveway that has started to fade, Latex-ite Airport Grade is the balanced choice: polymer-fortified, filler-rich and labeled for fuel resistance. For a rough driveway that has gone years without sealing, BLACK JACK Drive-Maxx 1000 is worth the premium. If price is the priority and you reseal every two years, Rust-Oleum EPOXYSHIELD Blacktop is the budget pick."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does the Menards 11% rebate apply to driveway sealer?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The 11 percent rebate promotions generally apply broadly across the store during the promotion period, and sealer has typically been included, but check the current offer's terms and exclusions. Remember the rebate arrives as a mail-in merchandise credit check usable at Menards, not cash. Timing a large sealer order to a rebate period can noticeably cut the cost of the job."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is coal tar sealer legal where Menards operates?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Minnesota banned coal tar sealants statewide in 2014, and a number of cities and counties in other Midwest states have local restrictions. Rules vary, so check with your city or county. Regardless of legality, asphalt-based sealers are the safer choice for families and pets and perform well when modified with polymer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use an asphalt sealer on my concrete driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. Asphalt emulsion sealers do not bond to concrete and will peel, leaving a patchy black surface that is hard to remove. Concrete needs a penetrating silane or siloxane sealer or a clear acrylic or waterproofing sealer. In salt country, penetrating sealers offer the strongest protection against scaling."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How many pails should I buy for a two-car driveway?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A typical two-car driveway is about 600 square feet. With a mid-range filler sealer covering roughly 250 to 350 square feet per pail per coat, two coats need about four or five pails, and premium heavy pails may need five or six. Weathered Midwest asphalt tends toward the higher number. Add one spare pail and keep it sealed for return."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "When is the best time to buy and apply sealer in the Midwest?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Buy in late spring or summer, close to when you will seal, ideally during a rebate period. Apply between late May and mid-September when air and pavement stay above about 50 degrees, nights are mild and no rain is forecast for at least a day. Avoid the hottest afternoons in July and August, when the sealer can dry before it levels."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I make a sealer job last through Midwest winters?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Fill cracks before sealing, apply two thin coats in good weather, and let the coat cure fully before traffic. In winter, raise snowblower skid shoes, use poly-edged shovels, ask plow services to lift the blade slightly, and use deicer sparingly. Each spring, fill new cracks and spot-treat worn areas before they spread."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
