              <a href="#winter-care" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Winter Care</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">13.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A Midwest driveway is a harder job than a Sun Belt one. Water gets into the pavement, freezes, expands and pries it apart every winter, and salt dragged in on tires speeds the damage. That shifts the buying priority toward flexible, polymer-modified sealers, careful crack repair before sealing, and coal-tar-free products. For most Menards shoppers with asphalt, Latex-ite Airport Grade is the balanced pick, and BLACK JACK Drive-Maxx 1000 is the premium choice for rough, older surfaces.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 5 Menards Driveway Products Worth Buying
          </h2>

          <p>
            We chose these from manufacturer labels and data sheets, the product type each is designed for, coverage and cure requirements, and patterns in owner feedback, weighted toward cold-climate performance. Menards stock changes by store and season, so check menards.com for your location before you go.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="midwest-factor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why a Midwest Driveway Changes What You Should Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            Menards operates across the upper Midwest and Great Plains, and that geography shapes the right sealer more than the store does. Winters bring dozens of freeze-thaw cycles, heavy plowing, and road salt carried onto the driveway on every tire. Those conditions punish brittle coatings and open cracks, and they reward products that stay flexible and builds that keep water out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Freeze-thaw is the main enemy. Water seeps into tiny surface cracks, freezes, expands by roughly nine percent, and wedges the asphalt apart a little more each cycle. A sealer that keeps water out of the top layer slows that process. A sealer that cracks with the pavement in January lets it continue. That is why polymer-modified products tend to be worth the extra cost north of roughly the 40th parallel.
          </p>

          <p>
            Salt adds a second problem. On asphalt, deicing salt itself is not especially damaging to the binder, but the extra melt-and-refreeze it causes near the surface multiplies the freeze-thaw cycles. On concrete, salt is a direct threat: it drives scaling and spalling, especially on slabs less than a year or two old. Your concrete choice should account for that.
          </p>

          <p>
            Snowplows and shovels are the last factor. Metal blades scrape off the top of any film, especially at the edges and on high spots. Expect wear there first, and expect to touch up the apron and the plow path more often than the rest of the driveway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coal-tar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Coal Tar Rules in Menards Country
          </h2>

          <p>
            Refined coal tar sealers were once the default in the Midwest because they resist fuel and wear well. They are also rich in polycyclic aromatic hydrocarbons, a group of compounds that includes several probable carcinogens. Research by the U.S. Geological Survey linked coal-tar-sealed pavement to elevated PAH levels in nearby house dust and stream sediment, and regulators responded.
          </p>

          <p>
            Minnesota banned the sale and use of coal tar sealants statewide in 2014. Before and after that, a number of local governments in the region adopted their own restrictions, with Dane County, Wisconsin, among the early ones. Rules differ town by town, so if you are outside Minnesota, check whether your city or county restricts coal tar products.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SAFE DEFAULT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Buy asphalt-based, coal-tar-free sealer.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Modern asphalt emulsions with polymer modification close most of the performance gap with coal tar, and they are far less toxic for kids, pets and the soil and storm drains your driveway runoff reaches. Read the safety data sheet if the pail does not say clearly.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            In practice, the major-brand pails on Menards shelves in restricted areas should already comply. The risk comes from contractor-grade products bought elsewhere or bulk sealcoat from a crew. If you hire someone, ask in writing what product they use, and see our explainer on <a href="/asphalt-sealcoating/">asphalt sealcoating</a> for the questions worth asking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="rebate-math" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Rebate Math: When the 11% Offer Changes the Choice
          </h2>

          <p>
            Menards runs its well-known 11 percent rebate promotions at intervals through the year. The rebate comes back as a mail-in merchandise credit check, which is only spendable at Menards, so it is a discount on your next trip rather than cash. For a driveway job that runs into several hundred dollars, that credit is meaningful.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rebate also narrows the price gap between tiers. If a premium pail costs noticeably more than a mid-range one, 11 percent back on a cart of five or six pails can cover a good part of the step up. If your driveway is rough enough to justify the premium product anyway, timing the purchase to a rebate period makes the decision easy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not let the rebate push you into buying in the wrong season. Sealer bought in late fall and stored through a Midwest winter is at risk of freezing, which ruins emulsions. Buy during a spring or summer rebate window close to when you will actually seal, and store pails indoors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the receipt and the rebate form together, and submit promptly; rebate offers have deadlines. Price matching policies exist as well, but terms change and exclusions apply, so ask at the service desk rather than assuming.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pail-count" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. How Many Pails Your Driveway Needs
          </h2>

          <p>
            Measure length times width for each paved area and add them. Divide by the single-coat coverage printed on the pail, then double it for two coats. Most filler sealers in 4.75-gallon pails list coverage somewhere around 250 to 400 square feet per coat; heavy premium pails are at the low end and thin emulsions at the high end.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Driveway</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Area</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Mid-range filler sealer, 2 coats</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Premium pail, 2 coats</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">One-car, short</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about 300 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 pails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3 pails</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two-car, typical</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about 600 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4-5 pails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5-6 pails</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Two-car, long</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">about 1,000 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6-8 pails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8-9 pails</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rural or turnaround</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500 sq ft and up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">9-12 pails</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">11-14 pails</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Midwest asphalt is often more porous than the label average because of the freeze-thaw wear described above, so lean toward the higher pail count. Then add one spare and keep it sealed. Returning an unopened pail is easy; driving back mid-job with half the driveway wet is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget for weight. Each full pail weighs in the neighborhood of 50 to 60 pounds, so a six-pail order is a real load for a small SUV. Menards' yard and pickup options can help with larger orders.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="concrete-aisle" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Concrete Driveways: Different Aisle, Different Rules
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many Midwest homes have concrete driveways, and they need a different product entirely. Black asphalt sealer will not bond to concrete and peels in sheets. For concrete you choose between a penetrating repellent, which soaks in and leaves no visible film, and a film-forming sealer like an acrylic, which sits on top and can add sheen.
          </p>

          <p>
            In salt country, penetrating silane or siloxane sealers are the strongest defense against scaling, because they keep salty meltwater out of the pores without creating a film that plows and tires wear through. Clear waterproofing sealers like SEAL-KRETE Original are a practical, widely stocked middle ground. For the full ranking, see our <a href="/best-concrete-driveway-sealer-reviews/">concrete driveway sealer guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing matters more on new concrete. Fresh slabs should cure for the period the sealer label requires before sealing, commonly about 28 days, and should be sealed before their first winter if possible. A first-winter slab exposed to salt without protection is the most likely to scale.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid using deicers based on ammonium nitrate or ammonium sulfate on any concrete driveway; they attack concrete chemically. Plain rock salt, calcium chloride or calcium magnesium acetate are gentler options, and sand gives traction without chemistry.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="short-season" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Working With a Short Sealing Season
          </h2>

          <p>
            In much of the Menards footprint, the realistic sealing window runs from late May to mid-September. Emulsion sealers need pavement and air temperatures of about 50 degrees and rising, overnight lows that stay mild, and a dry day or two. Spring often has warm afternoons but cold nights, and early fall can turn quickly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Late spring and late summer are usually the sweet spots. Mid-summer works if you avoid the hottest afternoons, when sealer can flash dry before it levels and leave streaks. Our guide to the <a href="/best-time-to-seal-driveway/">best time to seal a driveway</a> and the article on <a href="/what-temperature-to-seal-driveway/">what temperature to seal a driveway</a> go deeper on the numbers.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch for humidity as well as temperature. Midwestern summer humidity slows evaporation, which is how emulsions cure. On a sticky 85-degree day, a coat can feel dry on top and still be soft underneath at nightfall. Give it extra time before driving on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a fall forecast turns against you, waiting until spring is better than sealing into cold nights. A coat that cannot cure before the first frost may fail over the winter, and you will be redoing it anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep-list" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. The Prep Shopping List
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy the prep materials on the same trip. You need a stiff push broom, a leaf blower or shop vac, driveway cleaner or degreaser, an oil-spot primer, crack filler sized to your cracks, and a cold patch for any shallow holes. Our <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">crack filler rankings</a> compare the types.
          </p>

          <p>
            For application, pick up a squeegee or sealer brush, a trim brush for edges, a drill mixing paddle, and painter's tape or plastic for the garage threshold and walks. Gloves and old clothes are a must, because asphalt sealer stains permanently once dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the driveway needs pressure washing, a surface cleaner attachment keeps the result even and avoids the zebra stripes a wand leaves. Then allow at least a full sunny day for the surface to dry before sealing; trapped moisture is one of the most common causes of peeling.
          </p>

          <p>
            For oil stains, scrub first and only prime what stays tacky. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-remove-oil-stains-from-driveway/">removing oil stains from a driveway</a> covers cleaners that actually lift oil.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealing-day" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Sealing Day, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start once the dew has burned off and the pavement is warm. Tape the garage threshold and edges you want to protect. Stir the first pail thoroughly with a drill paddle, scraping the bottom, until it is uniform.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cut in along the garage, walks and edges with a brush. Then pour a ribbon of sealer across the driveway and pull it with a squeegee in overlapping passes, working in manageable sections from the garage toward the street. Keep a wet edge so you do not leave lap marks, and finish each section with strokes in one direction.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let the first coat dry until it is firm and no longer tacky, often a few hours in sun and longer in shade or humidity. Apply the second coat at a right angle to the first for even coverage. Do not try to save time with one heavy coat; thick coats cure poorly and track.
          </p>

          <p>
            Block the driveway entrance and keep vehicles off for at least a full day, longer in humid or cool conditions. For a deeper walkthrough, see our <a href="/how-to-seal-a-driveway/">guide to sealing a driveway</a>, and compare with the shelf at the other big-box chain in our <a href="/driveway-sealer-home-depot/">Home Depot sealer guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="diy-or-crew" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. DIY Pails or a Sealcoating Crew?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every spring, sealcoating crews canvass Midwest neighborhoods with flyers and door hangers, and it is fair to ask whether the pails are worth the effort. On a short one-car driveway, DIY almost always wins: a few pails, a squeegee and a Saturday cost far less than a contractor's minimum charge, which commonly lands somewhere in the low hundreds of dollars before square footage is counted.
          </p>

          <p>
            On long rural driveways and turnarounds the comparison tightens. Crews spray commercial emulsion from bulk tanks, often with sand and polymer mixed in on site, and their price per square foot drops as the area grows. Once you are looking at twelve or more pails, plus tools and two full days of hauling and spreading, a quote from a reputable crew may come in close to your material cost alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you hire out, vet the crew. Ask what product they use and get the answer in writing, confirm it is coal-tar-free, ask whether crack filling and oil-spot priming are included, and ask how many coats they apply. Be wary of door-to-door crews offering leftover material from a nearby job at a steep discount; that pitch is a common setup for a thin, watered-down coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            A sensible middle path is to handle crack filling yourself each spring, when cracks are small and cheap to fix, and decide each sealing cycle whether to do the topcoat yourself or hire it. Our guide to <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-seal-a-driveway/">driveway sealing costs</a> lays out typical contractor pricing so you can judge a quote.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="winter-care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. Protecting the Coat Through Winter
          </h2>

          <p>
            A good coat of sealer can still be shortened by rough winter care. Set the snowblower's skid shoes so the scraper bar rides slightly above the surface, and use a plastic-edged shovel or a plow blade with a rubber or poly cutting edge if you hire plowing. Ask your plow service to raise the blade a little on sealed asphalt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use deicer sparingly and sweep up the excess once the ice is gone. The goal is to break the bond between ice and pavement, not to bury the driveway in salt. Less salt means fewer melt-refreeze cycles at the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            In spring, walk the driveway and note where the coat has worn: tire paths, the plow line, and the apron. Spot-treat those areas the following season if the rest of the driveway is still in good shape. Our guide on <a href="/how-often-should-you-seal-your-driveway/">how often to seal your driveway</a> explains how to read wear patterns.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, fill any new cracks each spring before they widen. A few minutes with crack filler each year protects the base far better than waiting for the next full sealing. For a broader view of the products available beyond Menards, see our <a href="/best-asphalt-driveway-sealer-reviews/">ranked asphalt driveway sealers</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing over frost-opened cracks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every Midwest winter widens cracks, and sealer cannot bridge anything much wider than a hairline. Covering open cracks with sealer gives you a few weeks of good looks before they reappear and water keeps freezing in the base. Fill cracks first with a flexible filler and let it cure before sealing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying in fall and storing in the garage</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer bought on late-season clearance and left in an unheated garage can freeze over winter, and a frozen emulsion separates and is ruined. Buy close to when you will seal, store pails indoors at room temperature, and use them the same season.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Chasing the first warm weekend of spring</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A sunny 65-degree afternoon in April often comes with nights near freezing, and sealer cannot cure properly in that cold. Wait until overnight lows stay comfortably mild, usually late May in much of the region, before you open the first pail.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring coal tar restrictions</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Contractor or out-of-area products may still contain refined coal tar, which is banned in Minnesota and restricted in a number of towns elsewhere. Using it can mean fines and leaves high-PAH dust on the property. Check the safety data sheet and stick to asphalt-based sealers.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the plow blade ride on the surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Steel plow edges and snowblower scraper bars shave sealer off high spots and edges all winter. Adjust skid shoes, ask your plow service to lift the blade slightly, and use poly or rubber edges where possible. Your coat lasts noticeably longer in the plow path.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which Menards sealer should most Midwest blacktop owners choose?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a sound asphalt driveway that has started to fade, Latex-ite Airport Grade is the balanced choice: polymer-fortified, filler-rich and labeled for fuel resistance. For a rough driveway that has gone years without sealing, BLACK JACK Drive-Maxx 1000 is worth the premium. If price is the priority and you reseal every two years, Rust-Oleum EPOXYSHIELD Blacktop is the budget pick.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the Menards 11% rebate apply to driveway sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The 11 percent rebate promotions generally apply broadly across the store during the promotion period, and sealer has typically been included, but check the current offer's terms and exclusions. Remember the rebate arrives as a mail-in merchandise credit check usable at Menards, not cash. Timing a large sealer order to a rebate period can noticeably cut the cost of the job.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is coal tar sealer legal where Menards operates?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Minnesota banned coal tar sealants statewide in 2014, and a number of cities and counties in other Midwest states have local restrictions. Rules vary, so check with your city or county. Regardless of legality, asphalt-based sealers are the safer choice for families and pets and perform well when modified with polymer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use an asphalt sealer on my concrete driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Asphalt emulsion sealers do not bond to concrete and will peel, leaving a patchy black surface that is hard to remove. Concrete needs a penetrating silane or siloxane sealer or a clear acrylic or waterproofing sealer. In salt country, penetrating sealers offer the strongest protection against scaling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many pails should I buy for a two-car driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A typical two-car driveway is about 600 square feet. With a mid-range filler sealer covering roughly 250 to 350 square feet per pail per coat, two coats need about four or five pails, and premium heavy pails may need five or six. Weathered Midwest asphalt tends toward the higher number. Add one spare pail and keep it sealed for return.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is the best time to buy and apply sealer in the Midwest?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Buy in late spring or summer, close to when you will seal, ideally during a rebate period. Apply between late May and mid-September when air and pavement stay above about 50 degrees, nights are mild and no rain is forecast for at least a day. Avoid the hottest afternoons in July and August, when the sealer can dry before it levels.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I make a sealer job last through Midwest winters?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Fill cracks before sealing, apply two thin coats in good weather, and let the coat cure fully before traffic. In winter, raise snowblower skid shoes, use poly-edged shovels, ask plow services to lift the blade slightly, and use deicer sparingly. Each spring, fill new cracks and spot-treat worn areas before they spread.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

